00:09:53,960 --> 00:09:57,000
but he couldn't have done it
without the help of one person.

282
00:09:57,000 --> 00:09:57,760
That person

283
00:09:57,760 --> 00:09:59,680
(Marie-Madeleine)
was his second wife,

284
00:10:01,080 --> 00:10:02,480
Marie-Madeleine.

285
00:10:02,480 --> 00:10:05,320
When she married Nicolas Fouquet,

286
00:10:05,840 --> 00:10:08,680
she brought a very, very large dowry,

287
00:10:09,120 --> 00:10:13,080
and this dowry
was used by Nicolas Fouquet

288
00:10:13,080 --> 00:10:15,000
to purchase

289
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:17,320
this estate in 1641.

290
00:10:17,320 --> 00:10:19,960
But we know that in the end,

291
00:10:19,960 --> 00:10:21,960
Nicolas Fouquet faced a fate

292
00:10:21,960 --> 00:10:23,080
of being exiled.

293
00:10:23,080 --> 00:10:25,320
And his wife was implicated

294
00:10:25,320 --> 00:10:26,040
and exiled too.

295
00:10:26,440 --> 00:10:27,720
Ten years later,

296
00:10:27,720 --> 00:10:30,560
because of her tenacious character,

297
00:10:30,560 --> 00:10:33,680
she finally obtained
the forgiveness of Louis XIV,

298
00:10:33,680 --> 00:10:34,960
and ultimately, this estate

299
00:10:34,960 --> 00:10:37,400
was returned to the family.

300
00:10:38,920 --> 00:10:40,760
That woman was very, very great.

301
00:10:40,760 --> 00:10:41,240
Of course.

302
00:10:41,480 --> 00:10:44,720
She and her son returned to this estate

303
00:10:44,720 --> 00:10:46,600
and continued to live here

304
00:10:46,600 --> 00:10:47,720
until her death.

305
00:10:49,760 --> 00:10:51,200
The most beautifully decorated room

306
00:10:51,200 --> 00:10:53,200
is the next room we are heading to.

307
00:10:57,040 --> 00:10:58,840
Porcelain. This must be from China.

308
00:10:59,680 --> 00:11:01,240
Like an exchange. Yes.

309
00:11:01,240 --> 00:11:02,440
(17th-century Europe fell for Eastern culture.
Chinese porcelain signaled power and prestige)

310
00:11:02,440 --> 00:11:03,280
Looking up,

311
00:11:03,280 --> 00:11:05,760
we can see
a very, very beautiful ceiling.

312
00:11:05,760 --> 00:11:06,880
And this ceiling

313
00:11:06,880 --> 00:11:08,600
defines the name of this room.

314
00:11:08,600 --> 00:11:10,040
It is called the "Muses' Rotunda".

315
00:11:10,520 --> 00:11:12,800
You can all count carefully

316
00:11:12,800 --> 00:11:15,920
and see how many goddesses
are around in the circle.

317
00:11:17,920 --> 00:11:18,920
One, two, three...

318
00:11:19,840 --> 00:11:20,560
Eight?

319
00:11:20,560 --> 00:11:22,000
Yes, eight.

320
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:23,760
That means one is missing, right?

321
00:11:24,200 --> 00:11:24,840
Where did she go?

322
00:11:25,280 --> 00:11:27,800
In the center area.

323
00:11:27,800 --> 00:11:30,680
But not the person in the very center,

324
00:11:30,680 --> 00:11:32,960
but the one slightly lower down.

325
00:11:32,960 --> 00:11:34,760
Her name is Clio.

326
00:11:34,760 --> 00:11:36,120
Clio actually represents

327
00:11:36,120 --> 00:11:39,240
history among the nine Muses.

328
00:11:39,240 --> 00:11:41,640
And the person in the center
is not history

329
00:11:41,640 --> 00:11:42,240
or fame.

330
00:11:42,240 --> 00:11:42,840
What is she?

331
00:11:42,840 --> 00:11:43,920
She is loyalty.

332
00:11:44,400 --> 00:11:46,800
So this entire painting
is actually about

333
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:48,960
Nicolas Fouquet's loyalty to the king

334
00:11:48,960 --> 00:11:50,760
and the royal family.

335
00:11:50,760 --> 00:11:51,480
But even so,

336
00:11:51,480 --> 00:11:53,600
Louis XIV didn't buy it. Why?

337
00:11:53,600 --> 00:11:56,800
It actually has a lot to do
with the next room.

338
00:11:56,800 --> 00:11:57,720
It's about pledging loyalty.

339
00:11:57,720 --> 00:11:58,960
Like, "King, I understand.

340
00:11:58,960 --> 00:11:59,720
If I'm loyal to you,

341
00:11:59,720 --> 00:12:00,720
I will gain all this."

342
00:12:00,720 --> 00:12:01,320
The king didn't believe it.

343
00:12:02,160 --> 00:12:02,800
Why didn't he believe it?

344
00:12:02,800 --> 00:12:04,200
Because he only believed what one did,

345
00:12:04,200 --> 00:12:05,120
not what they said.

346
00:12:05,560 --> 00:12:06,800
And what did Fouquet do?

347
00:12:06,800 --> 00:12:08,320
He did this painting above.

348
00:12:08,960 --> 00:12:11,920
This is the strongman
from Greek mythology,

349
00:12:11,920 --> 00:12:13,080
Heracles.

350
00:12:13,520 --> 00:12:14,560
What does he represent?

351
00:12:14,560 --> 00:12:15,200
Power.

352
00:12:15,680 --> 00:12:18,280
So Louis XIV was unhappy.

353
00:12:18,280 --> 00:12:21,480
He was like, "Loyalty?
Judging by this painting,

354
00:12:21,480 --> 00:12:24,200
you're just clinging to power.

355
00:12:24,200 --> 00:12:26,720
You want to be second only
to the king, above all others."

356
00:12:26,720 --> 00:12:27,480
(Hold on)
But...

357
00:12:27,480 --> 00:12:28,960
I think he saw it backwards.

358
00:12:29,840 --> 00:12:30,880
He should see this first.

359
00:12:30,944 --> 00:12:31,880
(Listen to me)

360
00:12:31,880 --> 00:12:32,680
Right? Power.

361
00:12:32,680 --> 00:12:34,600
"I want financial power." And then...

362
00:12:35,440 --> 00:12:36,360
"Why do I want power?

363
00:12:36,360 --> 00:12:37,600
Because I want to be loyal to you."

364
00:12:38,840 --> 00:12:41,080
And where did his unhappiness

365
00:12:41,080 --> 00:12:42,440
actually erupt?

366
00:12:42,440 --> 00:12:44,440
It was in the next room.

367
00:12:45,080 --> 00:12:46,920
It's also the one we saw

368
00:12:46,920 --> 00:12:47,960
when we came in:

369
00:12:47,960 --> 00:12:49,880
a very, very large

370
00:12:49,880 --> 00:12:52,560
oval central hall.

371
00:12:53,080 --> 00:12:55,120
This central hall

372
00:12:55,120 --> 00:12:59,120
was where, on August 17, 1661,

373
00:12:59,120 --> 00:13:01,960
Nicolas Fouquet invited the royal family

374
00:13:01,960 --> 00:13:03,880
and very important nobles

375
00:13:03,880 --> 00:13:05,880
to come to Vaux-le-Vicomte

376
00:13:05,880 --> 00:13:08,840
for a very large celebration.

377
00:13:08,840 --> 00:13:11,120
And when Louis XIV came here

378
00:13:11,120 --> 00:13:13,600
and saw such a scene,

379
00:13:14,160 --> 00:13:15,440
he found it too perfect.

380
00:13:15,880 --> 00:13:18,160
So perfect that it made Louis XIV

381
00:13:18,160 --> 00:13:19,280
a bit unable to accept it.

382
00:13:19,280 --> 00:13:20,600
A bit jealous.

383
00:13:21,280 --> 00:13:22,760
After his jealousy arose,

384
00:13:22,760 --> 00:13:24,520
when he was leaving,

385
00:13:24,520 --> 00:13:26,000
he said to his mother,

386
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:28,240
Queen Anne of Austria,

387
00:13:28,240 --> 00:13:31,600
"Sooner or later,
I will make him spit out

388
00:13:31,600 --> 00:13:32,600
what he has swallowed."

389
00:13:33,080 --> 00:13:33,800
Because Fouquet was a noble

390
00:13:34,280 --> 00:13:35,400
and couldn't be killed.

391
00:13:35,400 --> 00:13:36,920
He could only be imprisoned for life.

392
00:13:36,920 --> 00:13:38,920
(Weeks later, Fouquet was arrested for corruption)
And his wife and children

393
00:13:38,920 --> 00:13:39,960
(His wealth seized, he was imprisoned for life)
couldn't even visit him.

394
00:13:39,960 --> 00:13:41,040
For ten years,

395
00:13:41,040 --> 00:13:42,320
they couldn't visit him.

396
00:13:42,320 --> 00:13:44,440
Was he released in the end?

397
00:13:44,440 --> 00:13:44,960
He was not released.

398
00:13:44,960 --> 00:13:46,800
He died in prison at the age of 65.

399
00:13:49,440 --> 00:13:51,360
What made Louis XIV truly jealous

400
00:13:51,360 --> 00:13:53,800
was actually not this castle...

401
00:13:53,800 --> 00:13:54,920
It was this garden.

402
00:13:54,920 --> 00:13:56,040
It was this very garden.

403
00:13:58,680 --> 00:13:59,800
Yes, I'm jealous too.

404
00:13:59,800 --> 00:14:01,560
Who wouldn't be jealous of this?

405
00:14:01,560 --> 00:14:03,080
This is too beautiful.

406
00:14:03,080 --> 00:14:03,600
Really beautiful.

407
00:14:04,600 --> 00:14:06,760
The builder of this castle

408
00:14:06,760 --> 00:14:08,200
(Castle Architect: Louis Le Vau)
was called Louis Le Vau.

409
00:14:08,560 --> 00:14:10,120
Almost all the ceiling paintings
we just saw

410
00:14:10,120 --> 00:14:12,080
came from the hand of one person.

411
00:14:12,080 --> 00:14:13,600
(Painter and Decorator: Charles Le Brun)
His name was Charles Le Brun.

412
00:14:13,600 --> 00:14:15,520
And the creator of this garden

413
00:14:15,520 --> 00:14:17,840
was called André Le Nôtre.

414
00:14:17,840 --> 00:14:22,160
After Nicolas Fouquet was arrested,
these three people

415
00:14:22,160 --> 00:14:24,160
were dispatched to Versailles

416
00:14:24,160 --> 00:14:28,200
to build that world-renowned
Palace of Versailles.

417
00:14:28,200 --> 00:14:29,640
So some say

418
00:14:29,640 --> 00:14:32,840
Vaux-le-Vicomte is the prototype
of the Palace of Versailles.

419
00:14:32,840 --> 00:14:33,840
And this garden

420
00:14:33,840 --> 00:14:36,280
was actually André Le Nôtre's

421
00:14:36,280 --> 00:14:37,600
first attempt

422
00:14:37,600 --> 00:14:38,760
at what we now know

423
00:14:38,760 --> 00:14:41,080
as the grand perspective.

424
00:14:41,080 --> 00:14:42,880
For the farthest point you can see,

425
00:14:42,880 --> 00:14:43,600
how many minutes

426
00:14:44,160 --> 00:14:46,160
do you think it would take you
to walk there?

427
00:14:47,160 --> 00:14:48,240
Twenty minutes.

428
00:14:48,240 --> 00:14:48,680
Ten minutes.

429
00:14:49,520 --> 00:14:50,800
It takes thirty minutes to walk.

430
00:14:50,800 --> 00:14:51,520
Look.

431
00:14:51,520 --> 00:14:52,760
It's very, very far.

432
00:14:53,040 --> 00:14:54,040
So the perspective

433
00:14:54,040 --> 00:14:56,440
is actually like a magic trick.

434
00:14:56,440 --> 00:14:59,000
It gives the illusion
that the end is near,

435
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:01,520
when in truth
it's a great distance away.

436
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:04,400
Nowadays,

437
00:15:04,400 --> 00:15:05,440
Vaux-le-Vicomte,

438
00:15:05,440 --> 00:15:06,840
with its palace architecture,

439
00:15:06,840 --> 00:15:08,640
French gardens, forests,

440
00:15:08,640 --> 00:15:10,120
and other diverse featured scenes,

441
00:15:10,120 --> 00:15:11,800
is not only a venue

442
00:15:11,800 --> 00:15:12,960
for various art exhibitions

443
00:15:12,960 --> 00:15:14,240
but also a favored filming location

444
00:15:14,240 --> 00:15:15,520
for numerous films and TV dramas.

445
00:15:15,840 --> 00:15:18,000
Just now, someone gave me a letter,

446
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:19,240
telling me to give it to you.

447
00:15:19,520 --> 00:15:20,840
And it's extremely urgent.

448
00:15:20,840 --> 00:15:22,840
You must read it right now.

449
00:15:25,240 --> 00:15:27,320
A sudden storm at Vaux-le-Vicomte.

450
00:15:29,520 --> 00:15:32,280
(The owner of Vaux-le-Vicomte
suddenly disappeared.)

451
00:15:32,280 --> 00:15:33,840
(The plot is a fictional dramatization)

452
00:15:33,840 --> 00:15:35,120
(Over the inheritance rights)

453
00:15:35,120 --> 00:15:36,440
(to Vaux-le-Vicomte,
his eight family members)

454
00:15:36,440 --> 00:15:37,440
(are at odds with each other.)

455
00:15:40,480 --> 00:15:41,520
(And today,)

456
00:15:41,920 --> 00:15:43,160
(a battle for the inheritance rights)

457
00:15:43,160 --> 00:15:44,560
(of Vaux-le-Vicomte)

458
00:15:44,840 --> 00:15:46,440
(is quietly beginning.)

459
00:15:46,440 --> 00:15:49,360
(The heir war for Vaux-le-Vicomte
begins now)

460
00:15:49,360 --> 00:15:51,080
My hat.

461
00:15:51,960 --> 00:15:52,800
Nobles are all like this.

462
00:15:52,800 --> 00:15:53,280
You have to hold it.

463
00:15:53,840 --> 00:15:54,320
Yes.

464
00:15:54,640 --> 00:15:56,920
Yes, that's true. They all hold it.

465
00:15:56,920 --> 00:15:58,240
This posture is very elegant.

466
00:15:59,080 --> 00:15:59,720
Yes.

467
00:15:59,720 --> 00:16:01,000
Yes, it's all like this.

468
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:01,720
Such a hat.

469
00:16:02,560 --> 00:16:02,880
Let's go.

470
00:16:07,200 --> 00:16:09,080
This is my favorite.

471
00:16:09,640 --> 00:16:10,480
Cheesecake.

472
00:16:11,520 --> 00:16:13,000
(Flan)
It's called Flan.

473
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:13,600
Flan?

474
00:16:13,840 --> 00:16:15,560
Your clothes are all replicas

475
00:16:15,560 --> 00:16:16,960
of 17th-century attire.

476
00:16:16,960 --> 00:16:17,880
Now you are

477
00:16:17,880 --> 00:16:19,720
17th-century French nobles.

478
00:16:19,720 --> 00:16:20,720
(Titled nobility)

479
00:16:20,720 --> 00:16:22,880
This is my favorite Flan.

480
00:16:22,880 --> 00:16:23,360
Yes.

481
00:16:27,960 --> 00:16:28,640
This is good.

482
00:16:29,480 --> 00:16:30,200
Is it sponge cake?

483
00:16:30,200 --> 00:16:30,640
Not bad.

484
00:16:31,680 --> 00:16:32,280
Sponge cake.

485
00:16:32,280 --> 00:16:33,720
Why does this look
so much like a mooncake?

486
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:36,160
This is especially like the churros

487
00:16:36,160 --> 00:16:37,520
we had in Spain, right?

488
00:16:37,760 --> 00:16:38,400
Toffee.

489
00:16:38,400 --> 00:16:39,320
It tastes like toffee.

490
00:16:42,480 --> 00:16:43,440
After arriving,

491
00:16:43,920 --> 00:16:45,120
I just want to eat hot pot mutton.

492
00:16:45,560 --> 00:16:46,840
We have mutton rolls on both sides.

493
00:16:46,840 --> 00:16:47,880
Look, here are two.

494
00:17:00,448 --> 00:17:02,080
(Vaux le Vicomte, Butler)

495
00:17:02,080 --> 00:17:02,880
Hello.

496
00:17:03,080 --> 00:17:04,040
Hello.

497
00:17:04,480 --> 00:17:05,839
Dear everyone.

498
00:17:07,760 --> 00:17:08,960
I feel like I can understand French.

499
00:17:09,160 --> 00:17:12,160
I am the butler most trusted
by the Viscount,

500
00:17:12,160 --> 00:17:13,916
(François Old Hand)

501
00:17:15,079 --> 00:17:16,119
Old hand.

502
00:17:16,119 --> 00:17:16,880
Old hand.

503
00:17:18,520 --> 00:17:20,520
Before the Viscount left,
he instructed me

504
00:17:21,079 --> 00:17:23,200
that an extremely reliable person

505
00:17:23,200 --> 00:17:26,520
must be found
to inherit Vaux le Vicomte.

506
00:17:26,784 --> 00:17:29,312
(The Succession Contest
of Vaux le Vicomte)

507
00:17:32,064 --> 00:17:32,960
(Claimed the title without hesitation)

508
00:17:32,960 --> 00:17:36,000
(Understood)
But he didn't specify who it should go.

509
00:17:37,680 --> 00:17:41,640
You are all the Viscount's
beloved family.

510
00:17:42,480 --> 00:17:44,640
Please discuss among yourselves first,

511
00:17:45,080 --> 00:17:47,720
and figure out
who is the most fit to be the heir.

512
00:17:47,880 --> 00:17:48,880
We can't reach an agreement
through discussion.

513
00:17:48,880 --> 00:17:50,080
(All ambitious contenders)

514
00:17:51,840 --> 00:17:52,920
It has been quite a while
since you last met.

515
00:17:52,920 --> 00:17:55,080
Let's introduce yourselves first.

516
00:17:55,320 --> 00:17:55,720
Go ahead.

517
00:17:55,720 --> 00:17:56,480
Okay.

518
00:17:57,920 --> 00:17:58,400
Start with me?

519
00:18:00,800 --> 00:18:02,840
I am the only

520
00:18:02,840 --> 00:18:03,616
true grandson here.

521
00:18:05,600 --> 00:18:07,240
He is the only real one?

522
00:18:07,600 --> 00:18:08,440
Grandson.

523
00:18:09,480 --> 00:18:11,360
So for the position of heir,

524
00:18:11,360 --> 00:18:12,840
I won't compete with you guys for now.

525
00:18:13,280 --> 00:18:13,840
Agreed.

526
00:18:13,840 --> 00:18:15,040
(Don't compete with me)

527
00:18:15,040 --> 00:18:15,960
Hello, everyone.

528
00:18:17,440 --> 00:18:19,680
I am the Viscount's only eldest son.

529
00:18:19,880 --> 00:18:21,240
The only eldest son?

530
00:18:21,240 --> 00:18:22,720
Then what are we?

531
00:18:22,720 --> 00:18:23,480
Daughters?

532
00:18:25,200 --> 00:18:26,520
Second son, third son.

533
00:18:28,120 --> 00:18:29,920
I don't plan to compete with you all

534
00:18:29,920 --> 00:18:31,480
(Sensitive)
for the inheritance.

535
00:18:31,880 --> 00:18:33,840
Please go easy on me.

536
00:18:33,840 --> 00:18:34,240
So magnanimous.

537
00:18:34,240 --> 00:18:35,227
(One less competitor)

538
00:18:35,240 --> 00:18:36,960
With my courage

539
00:18:38,920 --> 00:18:40,760
and wealth, I announce...

540
00:18:41,200 --> 00:18:42,240
Wait, who are you?

541
00:18:43,720 --> 00:18:44,160
I'm...

542
00:18:44,280 --> 00:18:44,960
Who are you?

543
00:18:45,400 --> 00:18:46,720
Who are you?

544
00:18:46,840 --> 00:18:47,920
Can you stand up?

545
00:18:47,920 --> 00:18:49,680
Given my seniority,
I don't need to stand up.

546
00:18:49,680 --> 00:18:50,880
(No pressure)
Because I am this castle's...

547
00:18:50,880 --> 00:18:51,800
(No favors)
Then get on your knees.

548
00:18:53,080 --> 00:18:55,200
I'm Viscount's elder brother.

549
00:18:55,200 --> 00:18:56,320
That means

550
00:18:56,320 --> 00:18:57,520
I am your uncle.

551
00:19:00,520 --> 00:19:01,360
My uncle?

552
00:19:01,360 --> 00:19:02,560
You know? Lamb hot pot.

553
00:19:04,560 --> 00:19:06,360
Yes. Watch your words
when you talk to me.

554
00:19:06,360 --> 00:19:06,760
Okay.

555
00:19:06,760 --> 00:19:07,360
That's enough. Next one.

556
00:19:07,360 --> 00:19:08,000
Your turn.

557
00:19:08,360 --> 00:19:08,720
You're done.

558
00:19:08,720 --> 00:19:09,360
Whose uncle are you?

559
00:19:10,200 --> 00:19:12,320
My identity is a long story.

560
00:19:12,320 --> 00:19:14,000
Then make it short.

561
